  {
    "id": "monsieur-leblanc",
    "name": "Monsieur Leblanc",
    "kind": "character",
    "firstChapter": 188,
    "importance": "supporting",
    "group": "Jean Valjean and Cosette",
    "lastChapter": 219,
    "mergedInto": {
      "id": "jean-valjean",
      "from": 220
    },
    "note": "The white-haired man Marius watches at the Luxembourg (Vol. III Bk VI–VIII, ch 188–219). Revealed as Jean Valjean in 'The Trap' (ch 220): Thénardier names him as the man who carried off the Lark from Montfermeil on Christmas eve 1823 (the reader saw that in ch 99–103). Use jean-valjean from 220; notes may say Marius still does not know who 'M. Leblanc' is. The narrator confirms it in his own voice at ch 234 and 237."
  },
  {
    "id": "the-girl-at-the-luxembourg",
    "name": "The girl at the Luxembourg",
    "kind": "character",
    "firstChapter": 188,
    "importance": "supporting",
    "group": "Jean Valjean and Cosette",
    "lastChapter": 219,
    "mergedInto": {
      "id": "cosette",
      "from": 220
    },
    "note": "Marius's 'Mademoiselle Lanoire' / 'Ursule' (ch 188–219). Revealed as Cosette in 'The Trap' (ch 220), where Thénardier calls the prisoner's daughter the Lark and Marius wonders 'if she is the Lark'. Use cosette from 220; confirmed at ch 237."
  },
